'' A STATIC ARRAY OF POINTERS INITIALISED FROM ADDRESSES is ONE RELOCATION PER ELEMENT,
'' and a MIR global carried exactly one. Measured -- fbc emits
''
''   static char*  A$[2] = { (char*)"a1", (char*)"a2" };
''   static tmp$5  F$[2] = { (tmp$5)&BAR, (tmp$5)&BAZ };
''
'' -- so neither could be expressed and both declined. The byte image the aggregate path
'' builds cannot hold an address at all, so it refused each word in turn.
''
'' The WORDS go in the pointer pool a vtable already uses. Nothing else about the global
'' changes: it is still a VAR, still writable, and the words are its initial value
'' rather than a table of its own. '@"a1"' is the literal's own global, which is where a
'' string literal already lives.
''
'' ALL OR NOTHING: a table is pushed only once every word is known, so a global is never
'' left holding a partial one with the rest silently zero.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/static-ptr-array.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: = [ @$str1, @$str2 ]
'' EXPECT-OUT: = [ BAR, BAZ ]
'' GOLDEN: expected/static-ptr-array.out
